WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.240
- [Narrator] These are bananas, obviously.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.920
They're from Trader Joe's.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.900
Today, they cost 19 cents
each, and about 25 years ago,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.380
bananas at Trader Joe's cost 19 cents.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.250
As of 2020, Trader Joe's gross
average sales per square foot

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.510
was more than double those

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.760
of other major grocery store chains,

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.370
and it's been ranked the
highest rated grocery store

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.560
for customer satisfaction.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.590
So how does Trader Joe's
manage to keep prices low

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.810
while out-competing
traditional grocery stores

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.433
by selling fewer products?

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.680
This is the economics of Trader Joe's.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.930
- Trader Joe's stores are smaller

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.050
and they tend to carry fewer items,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.640
and the vast majority of what
they sell is private label.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.370
- [Narrator] When the
store first opened in 1967,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.200
Trader Joe's founder, Joe Coulombe,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.930
emphasized stocking products
that were unconventional

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.800
to shoppers at the time like
Dijon mustard and granola.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.200
In 1979, Trader Joe's was bought

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.710
by the German discount grocery
store chain, ALDI Nord,

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.440
and today, Trader Joe's
has built its image

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.130
around selling trendy,
quirky, and seasonal items.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:11.970
- Orange chicken.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.120
- Cacio E Pepe pasta
sauce from Trader Joe's.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.540
- It helps with fueling the
experience for customers.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.460
People can go and kinda feel

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.830
like they're discovering something new.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.690
- [Narrator] And these
products have created

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.390
a large and loyal customer fan base.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.970
- I went to Trader Joe's
and I accidentally got,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.530
I got way too much stuff.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.450
- Of course, I love Trader Joe's.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.400
- [Narrator] Its
popularity on social media

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.990
is helpful for the company,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.290
because Trader Joe's doesn't
do a lot of advertising.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.390
Actually, the grocery store doesn't do

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.970
a lot of things that
other grocery stores do.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.630
There's no loyalty program,
no online shopping platform,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.210
and no deli counter, still. . .

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.750
- The Trader Joe's fan base is strong

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:59.610
and they are dedicated,
they are mega fans.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.460
People will sell Trader Joe's products

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.840
on places like eBay for
a much higher price tag

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.350
than what you would find at the stores.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:11.970
- [Narrator] Trader
Joe's smaller selection

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.910
can make the in-store shopping
experience more efficient

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.040
for customers, according to psychologist

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.140
and professor, Barry Schwartz.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.630
For example, instead of having to pick

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.240
between many different
brands of tomato sauce

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.640
at a conventional grocery store,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.670
customers at Trader Joe's
can quickly select one

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.890
from its more limited options.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.680
By selling a small
selection of house brands,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.320
Trader Joe's can control
its own supply chain,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.780
which it says brings down prices.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.020
- Trader Joe's items tend to be cheaper,

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.760
because they buy directly from
producers and manufacturers

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.730
whenever they can, so this cuts cost

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.340
that would typically go to the middleman,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.180
and the company says they
are able to pass that

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.580
along to shoppers.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.770
- [Narrator] Trader Joe's
says it buys in bulk

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.900
and often pays for items upfront

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.360
whenever possible from suppliers,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.490
this all helps lower prices.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.830
The grocery store chain also says it pays

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.790
for some products like Dijon
mustard in local currency,

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.770
so if the currency fluctuates,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.160
Trader Joe's takes on the
risk, not the supplier.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.800
And by assuming the
risk, Trader Joe's says

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.890
it can negotiate better
prices with suppliers.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:27.900
This control over its products

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.380
and supply chain means
Trader Joe's can easily add

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.684
and remove items from its shelves.

00:03:33.684 --> 00:03:36.120
- One of the unique
things about Trader Joe's

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.350
is they're constantly
changing their shelves.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:41.910
If products aren't selling

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.950
as well as they thought they would,

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.590
they remove them from their shelves

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.170
to make room for something better.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.490
- [Narrator] Some grocery stores

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:54.060
charge brands various marketing
fees to stock products,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.970
but Trader Joe's says it only makes money

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.220
when customers make a purchase.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.840
So according to Trader Joe's,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.690
this incentivizes it
to only stock products

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.280
that customers like,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.670
from Joe-Joe's Sandwich
Cookies to Kung Pao Chicken.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.360
- There isn't really another
chain that does what they do.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.170
There are other discounters
that sell for cheaper,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.410
but you're not gonna find the innovation

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.420
and the fun products there.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.200
The traditional grocery stores
have tried to go that route,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.971
but Trader Joe's is Trader Joe's.

00:04:27.971 --> 00:04:30.554
(upbeat music)

